\id 3JN - Restored Context Bible (RCB)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h 3 John
\toc1 The Third Letter of John
\toc2 3 John
\toc3 3Jn
\mt1 3 John

\imt Introduction
\ip The writer names himself only as "the elder" — the same way he opens 2 John, and for the same reason: the people receiving the letter know who he is and need no other name for him. His vocabulary is that of 1 John and the Gospel of John — truth, love, walking, bearing witness, doing good, being "from God" — and this letter belongs with those: same hand, same period, somewhere in the Greek-speaking world. He has standing across a group of assemblies rather than one. He sends men out, he expects them to be received, and here he writes to a man in one community about what is being done in another.
\ip It goes to Gaius, one of the commonest names in the Roman world, about whom nothing is known beyond what these fifteen verses say — that he has taken in travelling brothers he had never met, and that men coming back from those journeys reported it publicly. Unlike 1 John, which has no named sender, no addressee and no greeting, this is a personal letter with every part of one: sender, named recipient, opening wish for health, the business at hand, closing greetings. By word count it is the shortest book in the New Testament.
\ip The business is a quarrel over hospitality, and behind it a quarrel over authority. Travelling workers went out "for the Name" and would take nothing from outsiders, so they lived on what the assemblies gave them. Those assemblies met in private houses, which made taking such a man in one act of bed, board, funding for the next stage, and public backing. A man named Diotrephes, who in the elder's phrase loves to be first, will not receive the elder's men, talks against the elder, and throws out of the assembly anyone who receives them anyway.
\ip So the letter does three things in fifteen verses: it commends Gaius and tells him to keep on, it warns him about Diotrephes and promises a visit that will deal with him, and it vouches for a man named Demetrius — very likely the one carrying the letter to Gaius's door. Its companion, 2 John, tells an assembly whom not to take in. This one tells a man whom to take in. They are the same problem from opposite sides.

\c 1
\s1 Greeting, and Joy at the Reports of Gaius
\p
\v 1 The elder\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl ho presbyteros\tl*, with the article — not "an elder" but "the elder," the one man they know by that name and need no other for. He claims no office and offers no credentials. He opens 2 John the same way.\f*, to Gaius the beloved, whom I myself love in truth.
\p
\v 2 Beloved, in all things I pray that it may go well with you and that you may be in good health, just as it goes well with your life\f + \fr 1:2 \ft Letters of the day opened with a wish for the reader's health and prosperity, and this one keeps the form — but adds a measure to it. "Your life" is Greek \tl psychē\tl*, not a soul housed inside a man but the whole inner person, the living self. He has had a report of how Gaius stands inwardly, and prays that the rest may come up to it.\f*.
\v 3 For I rejoiced greatly when brothers came and bore witness to your truth \add [i.e. to the truth as they found it living in you]\add* — just as you yourself walk in truth.
\v 4 I have no joy greater than these things, to hear that my own children \add [i.e. those I brought into the faith myself]\add* are walking in the truth.
\s1 Keep Taking In the Travelling Brothers
\p
\v 5 Beloved, you are doing a faithful work in whatever you do for the brothers, and they strangers at that\f + \fr 1:5 \ft These were travelling workers sent out among the assemblies, and Gaius had never met them. Inns were costly, unreliable and often worse, so a traveller depended on being taken into a house — and since the assemblies met in houses, taking such a man in meant bed, board, funding and public backing all at once. Communities of this period were working out how to tell a genuine travelling worker from a fraud (compare Didache 11-13). 2 John is about turning the wrong ones away; this letter is about receiving the right ones.\f*,
\v 6 who bore witness to your love before the assembly \add [i.e. standing up and saying so in front of the congregation here]\add*. You will do well to send them on their way \add [i.e. to see them off supplied for the next stage of the journey]\add* in a manner worthy of God,
\v 7 for they went out for the sake of the Name\f + \fr 1:7 \ft Simply "the Name," with nothing added — the name of Jesus, so singular among them that it needed no qualifier. The apostles are said to have gone away rejoicing that they were counted worthy to be dishonored "for the Name" (Acts 5:41).\f*, taking nothing from the nations \add [i.e. accepting no support from the outsiders they go to, so no one can say they preach for pay]\add*.
\v 8 We ourselves, then, ought to hold up such men \add [i.e. take them in and keep them supplied]\add*, so that we may become fellow workers with the truth \add [i.e. so that what we give is as much a part of the work as what they do]\add*.
\s1 Diotrephes Refuses Them and Throws Out Those Who Do Not
\p
\v 9 I wrote something to the assembly\f + \fr 1:9 \ft He does not say what, and no letter we have can be identified with it. What matters to his point is not what he wrote but that Diotrephes would not have it.\f*, but Diotrephes, who loves to be first among them\f + \fr 1:9 \ft Greek \tl philoprōteuōn\tl*, a word found nowhere else — "loving to be first," made by joining the verb for holding first place to the verb for love. Paul uses that verb of the Christ, that in everything he might hold first place (Colossians 1:18). The charge is not that Diotrephes leads, but that he wants the place.\f*, does not receive us \add [i.e. will not accept me, nor anyone I send]\add*.
\v 10 For this reason, if I come, I will bring up the works he is doing \add [i.e. set them out in front of the whole assembly]\add*, chattering evil words against us; and not satisfied with these, he does not receive the brothers himself, and those who want to he stops, and throws them out of the assembly\f + \fr 1:10 \ft The word is the one used of the man born blind, whom they "threw out" of the synagogue for what he said about Jesus (John 9:34). To be put out of the assembly was to lose the only company these people had.\f*.
\s1 Copy What Is Good, Not What Is Evil
\p
\v 11 Beloved, do not imitate the evil \add [i.e. what you have just heard of Diotrephes]\add* but the good. The one who does good is from God; the one who does evil has not seen God\f + \fr 1:11 \ft His standing test, and the one 1 John runs throughout: what a man does shows where he came from (1 John 3:10). To have "seen" God here is not to have caught sight of him but to have met him and been changed by it. Placed where it is, straight after the paragraph on Diotrephes, the test returns a verdict — and he leaves Gaius to draw it.\f*.
\s1 Demetrius Is Vouched For
\p
\v 12 Witness has been borne to Demetrius\f + \fr 1:12 \ft Putting a man's character on record like this is a letter of commendation, standard business in the ancient world (2 Corinthians 3:1), and Demetrius is very likely the man carrying this one to Gaius's door. Everything the letter has said about receiving strangers comes to rest on him: receive this one.\f* by all, and by the truth itself \add [i.e. his life answers to the message, and that speaks for him]\add*; and we too bear witness, and you know that our witness is true.
\s1 He Will Come and Say the Rest in Person
\p
\v 13 I had many things to write to you, but I do not want to write to you with ink and reed pen.
\v 14 I hope to see you soon, and we will speak mouth to mouth\f + \fr 1:14 \ft The phrase is literal, and it is the one the Greek scriptures use of God speaking with Moses: "Mouth to mouth I speak to him, and in plain sight, and not in riddles" (Numbers 12:8). Speech with nothing standing in between. He closes 2 John the same way.\f*.
\v 15 Peace to you. The friends greet you\f + \fr 1:15 \ft "The friends" as a name for the community appears nowhere else in the New Testament letters. In John's Gospel Jesus tells the disciples, "You are my friends if you do what I command you... I have called you friends, because everything I heard from my Father I have made known to you" (John 15:14-15). And they are to be greeted one at a time, by name — in the same Gospel the shepherd calls his own sheep by name (John 10:3).\f*. Greet the friends by name.
